<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bophuthatswana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1977">1977</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bophuthatswana was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a> created by apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> for Tswana-speaking people, declared independent in 1977 but recognised by no country other than South Africa. It consisted of several separate enclaves, with its capital at Mmabatho, and was ruled by Lucas Mangope. After his overthrow in March 1994 it was reincorporated into South Africa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana.jpg" alt="Bophuthatswana" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Washiucho · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bophuthatswana_(1972%E2%80%931994).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area of former native reserves was set up as the only homeland for Tswana-speaking people in 1961 and administered by the Tswana Territorial Authority. It received nominal self-rule in 1971, and elections followed the next year. After the 1977 elections Chief Lucas Mangope became the first president when his Bophuthatswana Democratic Party won a majority. The territory became nominally independent on 6 December 1977. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> denounced the declaration as totally invalid, and Secretary-General Kurt Waldheim said he strongly deplored another so-called independent tribal homeland. Mangope argued that independence would let the population negotiate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> from a stronger position.</p><p>Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transkei">Transkei</a> recognised the state, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciskei">Ciskei</a> and Venda, the other members of the TBVC system. Even so, the government set up a trade mission in Tel Aviv and kept an unofficial embassy in Israel during the 1980s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> agreed to informal arrangements to ease cross-border travel. On 10 February 1988 Rocky Malebane-Metsing of the People's Progressive Party took power in a military coup and held it for one day; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force">South African Defence Force</a> invaded and reinstated Mangope. A second coup attempt in 1990 involved large protests against his handling of the economy, in which seven people were reported killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Bophuthatswana covered about 40,000 square kilometres and consisted of seven enclaves scattered across the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and Orange Free State, which led outsiders to call it Jigsawland. Mafeking was incorporated in 1980 after a local referendum. Townships such as Ga-Rankuwa and Mabopane, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, continued to serve as dormitory townships supplying labour. In 1990 its population was estimated at 2,352,296. Tswana, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> were official languages.</p><p>It was the richest of the TBVC states. Its platinum mines accounted for two-thirds of the platinum produced in the Western world, and it also had asbestos, granite, vanadium, chromium and manganese. The Sun City casino drew visitors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, where gambling was illegal. The rand remained the official currency, though two coins were minted as a proof set. Its defence force had about 4,000 troops and its police 6,002 officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In early 1994 Mangope resisted the coming elections and opposed reincorporation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Unrest grew, and he invited General Constand Viljoen of the Afrikaner Volksfront to help. Members of the Afrikaner Weerstandsbeweging also entered, shot at civilians and were driven out; three of them were executed after surrender, which ended white right-wing military opposition to the reforms. On 12 March 1994 the South African government and the Transitional Executive Council deposed Mangope. Bophuthatswana ceased to exist on 27 April 1994. Most of it joined North West province, Thaba 'Nchu went to the Free State and the Mathanjana exclave to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpumalanga">Mpumalanga</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bophuthatswana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grand apartheid assigned Africans to ten ethnic ‘homelands’, four of which, Transkei (1976), <b>Bophuthatswana</b> (1977), Venda (1979) and Ciskei (1981), were declared independent, although no other country recognised them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Free State used the turmoil to annex Thaba Nchu in 1884, though part of the land was later incorporated into the <b>Bophuthatswana</b> homeland.</blockquote><a href="/state/thaba-nchu">Thaba Nchu</a></li></ul></section>
